window.__DATA=window.__DATA||{};window.__DATA['bank/2024_06_2']={"id":"2024_06_2","title":"2024年6月 第2套","year":2024,"month":6,"set":"2","readingOnly":false,"hasAudio":true,"audio":"audio/304bf99f.mp3","audioDuration":1398.8,"audioMissing":false,"counts":{"total":55,"listening":25,"cloze":10,"matching":10,"reading":10},"passages":{"passage1":{"kind":"passage","text":"People often wonder why some entrepreneurs have greater success than others.Is it habits,connections, luck,work ethic or any other behavior?I believe the key to success is willpower.Willpower is the ability to control yourself.It is a strong determination that allows you to do something difficult.It is a behavior we are bornwith more than onewe learn;however,it is possible to not only learn it,but also strengthen it with constant exercise. Willpower isjust like a muscle;to keep it strong you need to constantly exercise it.People with a great amount of willpower have the discipline to develop positive,successful habits.Even with an incredible amount of talent,without the discipline and motivation to create positive habits,it can be difficult to achieve success. Willpower and habits go hand in hand.It is critical to create good habits and take the actions necessary to stick to those habits day in and day out for greater success.Almost half of our daily actions are part of our habits and not decisions,so once the correct habits are in place,you will automatically perform those tasks on a day-to- day basis. The best way to create and stick to a habit is to have strong motivation.It's easier to change your habits to lose weight if you have a health issue and you want to be around longer for your kids,or if your business is something you're passionate about.Having a valuable outcome associated with a habit will help you stick to that habit permanently. The art of self-control is one that most successful individuals have mastered.Self-control enables you to avoid behaviors that don't contribute to your success and adopt those that do. Because there is a delayed satisfaction associated with self-control,it can be easy to get off track.However if you work on sticking to those small positive habits one day at a time,it becomes easier to stay strong and achieve that delayed reward.Once a reward is achieved,it is much easier to continue sticking to your habits"},"passage2":{"kind":"passage","text":"Today,most scientific research is funded by government grants,companies doing research and development,and non-profit foundations.As a society,we reap the rewards from this science,but we also help pay for it.You indirectly support science through taxes you pay,products and services you purchase,and donations you make. Funding for science has changed with the times.Historically,science has been largely supported through private patronage(资助),church sponsorship,or simply paying for the research yourself.Today,researchers are likely to be funded by a mix of grants from various government agencies,institutions,and foundations.Other research is funded by private companies.Such corporate sponsorship is widespread in some fields.Almost 75% of U.S.clinical trials in medicine are paid for by private companies.And,ofcourse,some researchers today still fund small-scale studies out oftheir own pockets.Most of us can't afford to do nuclear research as a private hobby,but birdwatchers,rock collectors,and others can do real research on a limited budget. In a perfect world,money wouldn't matter—all scientific studies would be completely objective.But in the real world,funding may introduce biases.Drug research sponsored by the pharmaceutical(制药的)industry is more likely to end up favoring the drug under consideration than studies sponsored by government grants or charitable organizations.Similarly,nutrition research sponsored by the food industry is more likely to end up favoring the food under consideration than independently funded research. So what should we make of all this?Should we ignore any research funded by companies or special interest groups?Certainly not.These groups provide invaluable funding for scientific research.Furthermore,science has many safeguards in place to catch instances of bias that affect research outcomes.Ultimately,misleading results will be corrected as science proceeds;however,this process takes time.Meanwhile,it pays to examine studies funded by industry or special interest groups with extra care.Are the results consistent with other independently funded studies?What do other scientists have to say about this research?A little examination can go a long way towards identifying bias associated with the funding source."},"cloze":{"kind":"cloze","text":"with a single line through the centre.You may not use any ofthe words in the bank morethan once. A team of researchers led by Priyanka Joshi examined the degree to which men and women relied on “communicative abstraction”to verbally convey their ideas and emotions.Communicative abstraction, according to the researchers,reflects the tendency of people to use“abstract speech that focuses on the broader picture and26 purpose of action rather than concrete speech focusing on details and the means of 27 action.”Interestingly,they found that men were far more likely to speak in the abstract than were women. To arrive at this 28 ,the researchers examined the linguistic(语言的)patterns of men and women in over 600,000 blog posts written on websites.To do this,the researchers computed abstractness ratings for29 40,000 commonly used words in the English language.Words considered to be concrete could be easily visualized,such as “table”or“chair”.Words that were more 30 to visualize,for example, “justice”or“morality”were considered to be more abstract.They found that men used31 more abstract language in their blog posts. What is the 32 of this effect?The researchers suggest that power differences between the genders— that is,men having more power in society—might be a key determinant(决定因素).For instance,in a follow-up study conducted with a sample of 300 students,the researchers 33 power dynamics in an interpersonal setting to see if this would influence communicative abstraction.They found that participants in a high-power interviewer role were more likely to give abstract descriptions of behaviors than were participants in a low-power interviewee role.This suggests that communicating more abstractly does not reflect a 34 tendency ofmen or women but rather 35 within specific contexts.","wordBank":{"A":"approximately","B":"attaining","C":"conclusion","D":"difficult","E":"emerges","F":"fixed","G":"ignored","H":"legally","I":"manipulated","J":"recommended","K":"refraining","L":"signals","M":"significantly","N":"source","O":"ultimate"}},"matching":{"kind":"matching","text":"","paragraphs":{"A":"Hal Hershfield,a psychologist at the UCLA Anderson School ofManagement,wanted to know why people weren't saving for retirement.Across the board,people are living longer.Logically,they'll need more money to live comfortably in their post-work years.And yet,savings rates in the U.S.have gone down in recent decades,not up.","B":"To help explain this seemingly irrational behavior,Hershfield and his team scanned the brains of study participants while asking them to what degree various traits(特征)—like “honorable”or “funny”—applied to their current self,their future self,a current other,or a future other.As participants answered,Hershfield's team recorded which parts of their brains lit up.Unsurprisingly,people's brains were most active when thinking about their current selves and least active when thinking about a current other.But the team found that participants'brain activity while considering their future selves more closely resembled their brain activity while thinking about a current other rather than the current self.","C":"Put in practical terms,when thinking of yourself in a month or a year or a decade,your brain registers that person in ways similar to how it would registerTaylor Swift or the mailman.Understood in that way,saving for retirement is the equivalentofgiving money away to someone elseentirely","D":"In light of Hershfield's study,one simple question arises:Is it possible to make our present selves give a damn about our future selves?The answers are anything but simple.","E":"Seen through the lens ofthe present selfversus the future self,our self-defeating actions—like choosing to watch television rather than go to the gym—suddenly make perfect sense.We get to enjoy the very concrete, immediate benefits of our actions while someone else (namely,our future selves)suffers the hypothetical (假设的),far-off consequences.As a result,the decisions we make for our present selves often look very different from our decisions for our future selves.We believe that tomorrow.will be different.We believe that we will be different tomorrow;but in doing so,we prioritize our current mood over the consequences ofour inaction for the future self.","F":"Understanding our procrastination(拖延)through the lens ofthe present and future selves,we're left with three possible solutions:The first is to force your future selfto do whatever your present selfdoesn't want to do.The second is to convinceyour presentselfthat your future selfis,in fact,still you.Ifthe central problem is that we think of our future selves as other people,it follows that trying to identify more closely with our future selves will encourage us to make better long-term decisions.","G":"In a follow-up study,Hershfield wanted to explore ways to bridge the disconnect between the present and future selves and encourage people to save more for retirement.He and his team took photos of study participants,and then used image processing to visually age their faces.Participants were then placed in a virtual reality setting where they could look into a mirror and see their aged selves looking back at them. Participants who saw their aged selves said they would save 30%more oftheir salary for retirement than the control group.","H":"Whatever your long-term goals may be—getting in better shape,launching your own business,writing a book—thinking about your deadline in terms ofdays rather than months or years can help you wrap your mind aroundhow close the future really is.","I":"The third solution is to forget about your future self and use your present self's love of instant gratifcation (满足感)to your advantage.While the two tactics(手段)above can be effective in making better long-term choices,in the end,you're still struggling against human nature.Our brains are hard- wired for instant gratification.Instead of fighting your present self's need for immediate rewards,why not use it to your advantage?When most ofus set goals,we focus on long-term results we want to see— e.g.,losing weight,getting a promotion,retiring in comfort,etc.While those visions of our future selves can be inspiring,when it comes to actually doing the day-to-day work,it may be more effective to reframe activities in terms of their immediate,or at least very near-term,rewards.","J":"Take writing this article,for instance.It's easy for me to imagine how amazing it will feel at the end of the workday to have this article done.This isn't just my opinion.Research partners Kaitlin Woolley of Cornell University and Ayelet Fishbach ofthe University ofChicago have made a career out ofstudying the differences between the goals that people achieve and the ones that fall to the wayside","K":"“In onestudy,we asked people online about the goals they set at the beginning of the year.Most people set goals to achieve delayed,long-term benefits,such as career advancement,debt repayment,or improved health.We asked these individuals how enjoyable it was to pursue their goal,as well as how important their goal was.We also asked whether they were still working on their goals two months after setting them.We found that enjoyment predicted people's goal persistencetwo months after setting the goal far more than how important they rated their goal to be,”Woolley said.","L":"This pattern held true across a wide variety of goals from exercising to studying to eating healthier foods For example,people ate 50%more ofa healthy food when directed to focus on the good taste rather than the long-term health benefits.Other studies have shown a greater uptake ofexercise in people who were told to think ofthe enjoyment ofdoing the exercise now rather than future health gains. M)These findings suggest that when it comes to achieving your goals,enjoying the process itself is more important than wanting the long-term benefits.In other words,present selftrumps(战胜)future self.Who says instant gratification has to be a bad thing?By all means,set ambitiouslong-term goals for your future self,but when it comes to actually following through day-to-day,make sure your present selfknows what's in it for her too."},"letters":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G","H","I","J","K","L"]}},"questions":[{"no":1,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"1","options":{"A":"It hit a bird shortly after takeoff.","B":"Its crew members went on strike.","C":"It narrowly escaped a plane crash when turning around.","D":"Its captain got slightly injured during the forced landing."},"script":"[i]A JetBlue Airlines flight from West Palm Beach to New York City was forced to turn around and land Sunday\nmorning after the plane struck a bird. The flight from Palm Beach International Airport to LaGuardia Airport turned\naround just minutes after takeoff following the strike.\nNo injuries were reported on the plane, and the flight took off once again 7.5 hours after the first attempt. \"It was\nlike a split second of panic...that resulted in this nervous reaction on the plane,\" said passenger Brian Healy. [2] \"There\nwas total quiet, and then there was relief when the plane came to a stop.\"An email from JetBlue read, \"Our team is\nworking to accommodate customers on later flights.","audioStart":40.2,"groupNote":"Questions 1 to 2","answer":"A","expl":"It hit a bird shortly after takeoff. 详解：新闻开头提到，周日上午捷蓝航空公司一架从西棕榈滩飞往纽约的飞机在撞上一只鸟后被迫掉头降落，且飞机起飞后不久就发生了撞鸟事件。选项A与原文内容一致，其余选项（机组人员罢工、掉头时险些坠毁、机长受轻伤）新闻中均未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻细节捕捉，重点关注事件核心信息（时间、地点、事件经过）。"},{"no":2,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"2","options":{"A":"Panic.","B":"Nervous.","C":"Relieved.","D":"Contented."},"script":"[i]A JetBlue Airlines flight from West Palm Beach to New York City was forced to turn around and land Sunday\nmorning after the plane struck a bird. The flight from Palm Beach International Airport to LaGuardia Airport turned\naround just minutes after takeoff following the strike.\nNo injuries were reported on the plane, and the flight took off once again 7.5 hours after the first attempt. \"It was\nlike a split second of panic...that resulted in this nervous reaction on the plane,\" said passenger Brian Healy. [2] \"There\nwas total quiet, and then there was relief when the plane came to a stop.\"An email from JetBlue read, \"Our team is\nworking to accommodate customers on later flights.","audioStart":40.2,"groupNote":"Questions 1 to 2","answer":"C","expl":"Relieved. 详解：新闻末尾引述乘客布莱恩·希利的话：“那就像一瞬间的恐慌……造成飞机上乘客的紧张反应。人们完全安静了下来，当飞机停稳后才松了一口气。” 题目问的是飞机停稳后乘客的感受，“松了一口气”对应选项C（Relieved）。选项A（恐慌）、B（紧张）是事故发生后的第一反应，并非停稳后的感受，D（满足）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中人物观点及情绪捕捉，注意区分不同时间节点的情绪变化。"},{"no":3,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"3","options":{"A":"He is now kept in a secure area.","B":"He has escaped the zoo once again.","C":"He has been caught a second time.","D":"He finally disappeared six days ago."},"script":"A deadly snake, which had finally been tracked down after escaping a zoo, has slipped away for the second time.\nThe poisonous snake forced the closure of the attraction last week when staff noticed the disappearance.\nAfter six days of desperate searching, he was eventually found and placed in a supposedly secure area. [3] But it\nseems the animal is no fan of the zoo, because yet again he is out on the loose. [4] The snake is a relative newcomer to\nthe zoo but has already been frustrating its staff. The staff believe he squeezed himself out of a gap located around new\nenergy-saving bulbs installed inside the snake house. He was only found the first time around because staff brought in a\nspecial machine to trace him inside a wall opening.\nThis kind of snake is one of the most deadly and poisonous in the wild, and they can range from 3 to 5 meters in\nlength.","audioStart":149.9,"groupNote":"Questions 3 to 4","answer":"B","expl":"He has escaped the zoo once again. 详解：新闻开头提到，一条致命的蛇在逃离动物园后被找到，但又第二次溜走了。随后详细说明：第一次逃跑后经六天搜寻被找到并安置在安全区域，但再次出逃，且新闻未提及蛇被再次捕获。选项B符合原文，A（现在被关在安全区域）描述的是第一次被找到后的状态，非当前情况；C（再次被捕获）未提及；D（六天前最终失踪）表述错误，六天是第一次搜寻的时长，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻事件发展脉络梳理，注意时间顺序和事件状态变化。"},{"no":4,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"4","options":{"A":"Squeezed","B":"Threatened.","C":"Disappointed","D":"Frustrated."},"script":"A deadly snake, which had finally been tracked down after escaping a zoo, has slipped away for the second time.\nThe poisonous snake forced the closure of the attraction last week when staff noticed the disappearance.\nAfter six days of desperate searching, he was eventually found and placed in a supposedly secure area. [3] But it\nseems the animal is no fan of the zoo, because yet again he is out on the loose. [4] The snake is a relative newcomer to\nthe zoo but has already been frustrating its staff. The staff believe he squeezed himself out of a gap located around new\nenergy-saving bulbs installed inside the snake house. He was only found the first time around because staff brought in a\nspecial machine to trace him inside a wall opening.\nThis kind of snake is one of the most deadly and poisonous in the wild, and they can range from 3 to 5 meters in\nlength.","audioStart":149.9,"groupNote":"Questions 3 to 4","answer":"D","expl":"详解：新闻中提到“这条蛇对动物园来说是新来者，但它已经让动物园的工作人员感到挫败了”，选项D（Frustrated）与原文表述一致。A（Squeezed）是描述蛇逃跑的方式（挤进缝隙），并非工作人员的感受；B（受到威胁的）、C（失望的）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中人物感受捕捉，注意区分描述事件的词汇和表达感受的词汇。"},{"no":5,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"5","options":{"A":"It is condemned as a crazy idea.","B":"It is enriching the city's night life.","C":"It is questioned by local residents.","D":"It is giving rise to safety concerns."},"script":"Electric bikes have been the craze in downtown Jacksonville since they were first introduced earlier this month as\na one-year pilot program. [5] But they 're leading to safety concerns, mainly at night when some riders don't follow the\nrules of the road.\nAs the night goes on, groups of riders are often seen traveling in just about every direction in the streets and on\npedestrian paths.That is increasing the probability of dangerous accidents.\n[6] Electric bike riders have to follow all the same rules as you would if you were in an automobile. That means\nno running red lights or traveling in the opposite direction of traffic on one-way streets.\n[7] City Council Member Anna Cumber was instrumental in introducing the electric bikes to Jacksonville as a way to\nbring new life into downtown. And she's aware of the concerns. Cumber said, \"People can have fun, but both drivers and\nriders are responsible for paying attention while on the road.And never assume the driver is going to stop or see you.\"","audioStart":265.5,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"D","expl":"It is giving rise to safety concerns. 详解：新闻开头提到，杰克逊维尔市中心推出电动自行车试点项目后掀起热潮，但也引发了安全担忧，尤其是夜间部分骑行者不遵守交通规则。选项D（引发安全担忧）与原文一致。A（被指责为疯狂想法）、B（丰富城市夜生活）未提及；C（受到当地居民质疑）错误，原文仅说有安全担忧，未提及质疑项目本身，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻核心问题及影响捕捉，注意转折连词后的关键信息。"},{"no":6,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"6","options":{"A":"Avoid entering one-way streets","B":"Ensure the safety of pedestrians.","C":"Follow all the traffic rules drivers do.","D":"Give way to automobiles at all times."},"script":"Electric bikes have been the craze in downtown Jacksonville since they were first introduced earlier this month as\na one-year pilot program. [5] But they 're leading to safety concerns, mainly at night when some riders don't follow the\nrules of the road.\nAs the night goes on, groups of riders are often seen traveling in just about every direction in the streets and on\npedestrian paths.That is increasing the probability of dangerous accidents.\n[6] Electric bike riders have to follow all the same rules as you would if you were in an automobile. That means\nno running red lights or traveling in the opposite direction of traffic on one-way streets.\n[7] City Council Member Anna Cumber was instrumental in introducing the electric bikes to Jacksonville as a way to\nbring new life into downtown. And she's aware of the concerns. Cumber said, \"People can have fun, but both drivers and\nriders are responsible for paying attention while on the road.And never assume the driver is going to stop or see you.\"","audioStart":265.5,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"C","expl":"Follow all the traffic rules drivers do. 详解：新闻中明确提到“电动自行车骑行者必须遵守和开车一样的规则。这意味着不能闯红灯，也不能在单行道上逆行”，选项C与原文表述一致。A（避免进入单行道）错误，原文是不能逆行，并非不能进入；B（确保行人安全）、D（任何时候给汽车让路）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中规则类信息捕捉，注意同义替换（follow all the same rules as automobile drivers = follow all the traffic rules drivers do）。"},{"no":7,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"7","options":{"A":"To ease the city's busy traffic.","B":"To bring new life into the city.","C":"To add a new means of transport.","D":"To reduce the city's air pollution."},"script":"Electric bikes have been the craze in downtown Jacksonville since they were first introduced earlier this month as\na one-year pilot program. [5] But they 're leading to safety concerns, mainly at night when some riders don't follow the\nrules of the road.\nAs the night goes on, groups of riders are often seen traveling in just about every direction in the streets and on\npedestrian paths.That is increasing the probability of dangerous accidents.\n[6] Electric bike riders have to follow all the same rules as you would if you were in an automobile. That means\nno running red lights or traveling in the opposite direction of traffic on one-way streets.\n[7] City Council Member Anna Cumber was instrumental in introducing the electric bikes to Jacksonville as a way to\nbring new life into downtown. And she's aware of the concerns. Cumber said, \"People can have fun, but both drivers and\nriders are responsible for paying attention while on the road.And never assume the driver is going to stop or see you.\"","audioStart":265.5,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"B","expl":"To bring new life into the city. 详解：新闻末尾提到“市议员安娜·坎伯在将电动自行车引入杰克逊维尔，为市中心带来新的活力方面发挥了重要作用”，选项B与原文一致。A（缓解交通拥堵）、C（增加新交通工具）、D（减少空气污染）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中事件目的捕捉，关注人物的核心诉求表述。"},{"no":8,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"8","options":{"A":"Take it seriously.","B":"Discuss it regularly.","C":"Make it sound scary.","D":"Talk about it openly."},"script":"M: What's the best way to teach children how to save and spend their money?\nW: [8] You should make money a regular topic of discussion. It's best to start young, so it's instinctive rather than a\nscary subject.\nM: In our family, we talk openly about things like the budget for holidays, how taxes reduce your income, and how to\nshop around for the best deals.\nW: Indeed. [9] It's also essential to make money real for children through practical examples. Working out how much\nwe save using discount pizza coupons, for example, is much more relevant than abstract sums.\nM: [1o] We also give our kids pocket money, and the amount they get is linked to chores, such as putting the bins out\nand emptying the dishwasher.\nW: [io] We do that too, and it's paid according to their age. Two pounds for each year so they can see some\nprogression.\nM: Teaching them to save is important. We opened a savings account when they were young. After birthdays and\nChristmas, they would go to the branch and deposit their gift money.\nW: Oh, I hadn't considered doing that. In our house, we have transparent money boxes for them to put small change in.\nso they can see their savings grow.\nM: When the time is right, I'll start talking to our children about investing and show them how the money saved for\ntheir further education has grown.\nW: [11] I am always talking to my elder daughter about the importance of saving into a pension, She's just started a\npart-time job and was thinking of not contributing to her pension. Luckily, I managed to persuade her otherwise.\nM: Yes, it's such an important lesson to learn.","audioStart":445.5,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"B","expl":"Discuss it regularly. 详解：对话开头男士询问教孩子存钱和消费的最佳方法，女士回答“你应该把钱作为一个经常讨论的话题。最好从年轻时开始，这样它就会成为本能，而不是一个可怕的话题”。选项B（经常讨论它）与原文一致。A（认真对待它）未提及；C（让它听起来吓人）与女士观点相反；D（公开谈论它）是男士家的做法，并非女士的建议，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中人物建议捕捉，注意区分不同说话者的观点。"},{"no":9,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"9","options":{"A":"Byshopping around for the best deals.","B":"By making abstract sums relevant.","C":"By citing concrete examples","D":"By visiting discount stores."},"script":"M: What's the best way to teach children how to save and spend their money?\nW: [8] You should make money a regular topic of discussion. It's best to start young, so it's instinctive rather than a\nscary subject.\nM: In our family, we talk openly about things like the budget for holidays, how taxes reduce your income, and how to\nshop around for the best deals.\nW: Indeed. [9] It's also essential to make money real for children through practical examples. Working out how much\nwe save using discount pizza coupons, for example, is much more relevant than abstract sums.\nM: [1o] We also give our kids pocket money, and the amount they get is linked to chores, such as putting the bins out\nand emptying the dishwasher.\nW: [io] We do that too, and it's paid according to their age. Two pounds for each year so they can see some\nprogression.\nM: Teaching them to save is important. We opened a savings account when they were young. After birthdays and\nChristmas, they would go to the branch and deposit their gift money.\nW: Oh, I hadn't considered doing that. In our house, we have transparent money boxes for them to put small change in.\nso they can see their savings grow.\nM: When the time is right, I'll start talking to our children about investing and show them how the money saved for\ntheir further education has grown.\nW: [11] I am always talking to my elder daughter about the importance of saving into a pension, She's just started a\npart-time job and was thinking of not contributing to her pension. Luckily, I managed to persuade her otherwise.\nM: Yes, it's such an important lesson to learn.","audioStart":445.5,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"C","expl":"By citing concrete examples. 详解：女士提到“通过实际例子让孩子们真正了解钱也很重要，比如算出使用披萨折扣券能省多少钱，这比抽象的数字更有意义”，选项C（列举具体例子）与原文一致。A（货比三家找最佳优惠）是男士家的做法；B（让抽象金额变得有意义）是例子的作用，而非方法本身；D（逛折扣店）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中具体方法捕捉，注意例子与核心方法的关联。"},{"no":10,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"10","options":{"A":"Paying their kids to help with housework","B":"Setting kids a good example by sharing chores","C":"Giving kidspocket money according to their needs.","D":"Urging kids to deposit some oftheir gift money."},"script":"M: What's the best way to teach children how to save and spend their money?\nW: [8] You should make money a regular topic of discussion. It's best to start young, so it's instinctive rather than a\nscary subject.\nM: In our family, we talk openly about things like the budget for holidays, how taxes reduce your income, and how to\nshop around for the best deals.\nW: Indeed. [9] It's also essential to make money real for children through practical examples. Working out how much\nwe save using discount pizza coupons, for example, is much more relevant than abstract sums.\nM: [1o] We also give our kids pocket money, and the amount they get is linked to chores, such as putting the bins out\nand emptying the dishwasher.\nW: [io] We do that too, and it's paid according to their age. Two pounds for each year so they can see some\nprogression.\nM: Teaching them to save is important. We opened a savings account when they were young. After birthdays and\nChristmas, they would go to the branch and deposit their gift money.\nW: Oh, I hadn't considered doing that. In our house, we have transparent money boxes for them to put small change in.\nso they can see their savings grow.\nM: When the time is right, I'll start talking to our children about investing and show them how the money saved for\ntheir further education has grown.\nW: [11] I am always talking to my elder daughter about the importance of saving into a pension, She's just started a\npart-time job and was thinking of not contributing to her pension. Luckily, I managed to persuade her otherwise.\nM: Yes, it's such an important lesson to learn.","audioStart":445.5,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"A","expl":"Paying their kids to help with housework. 详解：男士提到“我们给孩子零花钱，数额与家务挂钩，比如倒垃圾、清空洗碗机”，女士回应“我们也是这么做的，而且按年龄支付”，说明两人的共同做法是给做家务的孩子付零花钱，选项A符合原文。B（通过分担家务树立榜样）未提及；C（按孩子需求给零花钱）与原文“按年龄”“与家务挂钩”不符；D（敦促孩子存红包钱）是男士家的做法，女士未采用，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中共同信息捕捉，注意双方观点的交集。"},{"no":11,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"11","options":{"A":"The importance of cutting down family expenses.","B":"The need to learn important lessons from her elders.","C":"The delight in seeing their savings grow.","D":"The necessity of saving into a pension."},"script":"M: What's the best way to teach children how to save and spend their money?\nW: [8] You should make money a regular topic of discussion. It's best to start young, so it's instinctive rather than a\nscary subject.\nM: In our family, we talk openly about things like the budget for holidays, how taxes reduce your income, and how to\nshop around for the best deals.\nW: Indeed. [9] It's also essential to make money real for children through practical examples. Working out how much\nwe save using discount pizza coupons, for example, is much more relevant than abstract sums.\nM: [1o] We also give our kids pocket money, and the amount they get is linked to chores, such as putting the bins out\nand emptying the dishwasher.\nW: [io] We do that too, and it's paid according to their age. Two pounds for each year so they can see some\nprogression.\nM: Teaching them to save is important. We opened a savings account when they were young. After birthdays and\nChristmas, they would go to the branch and deposit their gift money.\nW: Oh, I hadn't considered doing that. In our house, we have transparent money boxes for them to put small change in.\nso they can see their savings grow.\nM: When the time is right, I'll start talking to our children about investing and show them how the money saved for\ntheir further education has grown.\nW: [11] I am always talking to my elder daughter about the importance of saving into a pension, She's just started a\npart-time job and was thinking of not contributing to her pension. Luckily, I managed to persuade her otherwise.\nM: Yes, it's such an important lesson to learn.","audioStart":445.5,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"D","expl":"The necessity of saving into a pension. 详解：对话末尾女士提到“我经常和大女儿谈论储蓄养老金的重要性，她刚开始兼职，本不想缴纳养老金，幸好我说服了她”，选项D与原文一致。A（减少家庭开支的重要性）、B（向长辈汲取经验的必要性）未提及；C（看到存款增长的喜悦）是储蓄的结果，并非谈论的核心话题，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中核心话题捕捉，关注人物反复强调的内容。"},{"no":12,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"12","options":{"A":"He is a successful businessman.","B":"He reviews books on political affairs.","C":"He has published a new bestseller.","D":"He has worked in several banks."},"script":"W: Welcome to Books in Review. [1z] Our guest today is John Banks, the author of the best selling new book,\nRewarding Success.\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\nW: Your book has sold 2 million copies, but before we discuss why it's getting so much attention, let's talk about your\nbackground. You're an economist and spent two decades teaching at universities?\nM: I spent 25 years as a professor, actually. And then, for the last 10 years, I've worked as a political consultant,\nadvising politicians at the national level about problems in our country.\nW: You discuss three of those problems in the book, improving public education, reducing our nation's healthcare\nburden, and increasing personal savings. [13] But your ideas about education are the most controversial.\nM: Absolutely.A lot of people think I'm trying to punish students who aren't doing well, [14] when actually my goal is\nto give all students more incentive to succeed.\nW: I'm not sure I agree with you. Your proposal to pay cash rewards to students who get good grades is a particular\nproblem. What about students who don't get good grades? It seems like you're blaming them for not succeeding.\nwhen poor performance in school isn't a child's fault.\nM: But my proposal is not just to reward students with good grades, but also students who show improvements.\nW: Okay, another criticism of the plan is the cash rewards themselves. Where will the money come from?\nM: If students do better, we'll spend less on schooling- So, in the end, the rewards will pay for themselves.\nW: What about now? How will we fund the rewards in the meantime?\nM: Well, [15] by increasing taxes or moving money from other areas of the budget into education.","audioStart":634.0,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"C","expl":"详解：对话开头女士介绍“我们今天的嘉宾是约翰·班克斯，畅销新书《奖励成功》的作者”，选项C与原文一致。A（成功商人）错误，男士是经济学家、前大学教授、政治顾问；B（评论政治事务书籍）未提及；D（在多家银行工作）是对名字“Banks”的误解，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中人物背景信息捕捉，注意核心身份描述。"},{"no":13,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"13","options":{"A":"The man's ideas about education","B":"The man's academic background.","C":"The man's attempts at drawing public attention.","D":"The man's proposals to solve economic problems"},"script":"W: Welcome to Books in Review. [1z] Our guest today is John Banks, the author of the best selling new book,\nRewarding Success.\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\nW: Your book has sold 2 million copies, but before we discuss why it's getting so much attention, let's talk about your\nbackground. You're an economist and spent two decades teaching at universities?\nM: I spent 25 years as a professor, actually. And then, for the last 10 years, I've worked as a political consultant,\nadvising politicians at the national level about problems in our country.\nW: You discuss three of those problems in the book, improving public education, reducing our nation's healthcare\nburden, and increasing personal savings. [13] But your ideas about education are the most controversial.\nM: Absolutely.A lot of people think I'm trying to punish students who aren't doing well, [14] when actually my goal is\nto give all students more incentive to succeed.\nW: I'm not sure I agree with you. Your proposal to pay cash rewards to students who get good grades is a particular\nproblem. What about students who don't get good grades? It seems like you're blaming them for not succeeding.\nwhen poor performance in school isn't a child's fault.\nM: But my proposal is not just to reward students with good grades, but also students who show improvements.\nW: Okay, another criticism of the plan is the cash rewards themselves. Where will the money come from?\nM: If students do better, we'll spend less on schooling- So, in the end, the rewards will pay for themselves.\nW: What about now? How will we fund the rewards in the meantime?\nM: Well, [15] by increasing taxes or moving money from other areas of the budget into education.","audioStart":634.0,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"A","expl":"The man's ideas about education. 详解：女士提到“你在书中讨论了三个问题：改善公共教育、减轻医疗负担、增加个人储蓄，但你关于教育的观点最具争议性”，选项A与原文一致。B（男士的学术背景）、C（男士试图吸引公众注意）、D（男士解决经济问题的建议）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中争议性话题捕捉，注意转折连词后的关键信息。"},{"no":14,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"14","options":{"A":"To reduce students'financial burden.","B":"To motivate all students to be successful.","C":"To give students incentive to excel in economics.","D":"To provide remedies for students'poor performance"},"script":"W: Welcome to Books in Review. [1z] Our guest today is John Banks, the author of the best selling new book,\nRewarding Success.\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\nW: Your book has sold 2 million copies, but before we discuss why it's getting so much attention, let's talk about your\nbackground. You're an economist and spent two decades teaching at universities?\nM: I spent 25 years as a professor, actually. And then, for the last 10 years, I've worked as a political consultant,\nadvising politicians at the national level about problems in our country.\nW: You discuss three of those problems in the book, improving public education, reducing our nation's healthcare\nburden, and increasing personal savings. [13] But your ideas about education are the most controversial.\nM: Absolutely.A lot of people think I'm trying to punish students who aren't doing well, [14] when actually my goal is\nto give all students more incentive to succeed.\nW: I'm not sure I agree with you. Your proposal to pay cash rewards to students who get good grades is a particular\nproblem. What about students who don't get good grades? It seems like you're blaming them for not succeeding.\nwhen poor performance in school isn't a child's fault.\nM: But my proposal is not just to reward students with good grades, but also students who show improvements.\nW: Okay, another criticism of the plan is the cash rewards themselves. Where will the money come from?\nM: If students do better, we'll spend less on schooling- So, in the end, the rewards will pay for themselves.\nW: What about now? How will we fund the rewards in the meantime?\nM: Well, [15] by increasing taxes or moving money from other areas of the budget into education.","audioStart":634.0,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"B","expl":"详解：男士回应争议时提到“很多人认为我在惩罚成绩不好的学生，但实际上我的目标是给所有学生更多成功的动力”，选项B与原文一致。A（减轻学生经济负担）、C（激励学生在经济学上取得优异成绩）、D（为学生糟糕成绩提供补救措施）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中人物真实意图捕捉，注意区分他人误解与真实目的。"},{"no":15,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"15","options":{"A":"Improving school budgeting.","B":"Increasing tuition fees.","C":"Seeking donations.","D":"Raising taxes."},"script":"W: Welcome to Books in Review. [1z] Our guest today is John Banks, the author of the best selling new book,\nRewarding Success.\nM: Glad to be here, Jane.\nW: Your book has sold 2 million copies, but before we discuss why it's getting so much attention, let's talk about your\nbackground. You're an economist and spent two decades teaching at universities?\nM: I spent 25 years as a professor, actually. And then, for the last 10 years, I've worked as a political consultant,\nadvising politicians at the national level about problems in our country.\nW: You discuss three of those problems in the book, improving public education, reducing our nation's healthcare\nburden, and increasing personal savings. [13] But your ideas about education are the most controversial.\nM: Absolutely.A lot of people think I'm trying to punish students who aren't doing well, [14] when actually my goal is\nto give all students more incentive to succeed.\nW: I'm not sure I agree with you. Your proposal to pay cash rewards to students who get good grades is a particular\nproblem. What about students who don't get good grades? It seems like you're blaming them for not succeeding.\nwhen poor performance in school isn't a child's fault.\nM: But my proposal is not just to reward students with good grades, but also students who show improvements.\nW: Okay, another criticism of the plan is the cash rewards themselves. Where will the money come from?\nM: If students do better, we'll spend less on schooling- So, in the end, the rewards will pay for themselves.\nW: What about now? How will we fund the rewards in the meantime?\nM: Well, [15] by increasing taxes or moving money from other areas of the budget into education.","audioStart":634.0,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"D","expl":"Raising taxes. 详解：女士询问奖励资金的来源，男士回答“通过增加税收或将预算中其他领域的资金转入教育”，选项D（增加税收）是其中一种方式，与原文一致。A（改进学校预算）、B（增加学费）、C（寻求捐助）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中具体方案捕捉，注意列举的关键信息。"},{"no":16,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"16","options":{"A":"To show his sympathy towards people always being busy.","B":"To prevent people from complaining about being“busy”.","C":"To call on busy people to take care ofthemselves.","D":"To help busy people to sort out their priorities."},"script":"I met three different people today, and each time, when I asked, \"How are you?\" The reply was exactly the same.\n**I'm busy\".Honestly, I hear the same answer from the vast majority of people I meet. So I started to think: \"Guess\nwhat? Everybody's busy!\" I'm busy, you're busy, everybody's busy. So you being busy doesn't make me sympathetic\nat all.Because\"busy\"is the state of the world.\n[16] So I'm launching a campaign to stop people complaining about being \"busy.\" It may sound harsh, but the truth is, nobody cares. In a busy world, \"being busy\" doesn't stand out, nor does it mean productive, creative,\naccomplished,or professional.\nSteve Maraboli once said, *When someone tells you they are too \"busy,it's not a reflection of their schedule: it's\na reflection of YOUR spot on their schedule.\"Donna Lynn Hope has also remarked, \"Busy doesn't mean better. I've\nnever envied a busy person—the one who likes to point out their busy lives to others.\"\nThe reality is, nobody is too busy-it's just about priorities. [17] So the next time someone asks you how you are,\nmaybe respond differently.[18] In my experience, the \"I'm busy\"response is really covering up the fact that they're not\nactually accomplishing their real purpose. And \"being busy\"is the lie they tell themselves about why they can't achieve it.","audioStart":867.8,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"B","expl":"详解：短文开头提到“我发起了一场运动，让人们停止抱怨‘忙碌’。说实话，没人在乎你忙不忙，在这个人人都忙的世界里，‘忙碌’既不突出，也不意味着高效、有创造力”，选项B与原文一致。A（同情总是忙碌的人）与原文“忙碌不会让我产生同情”相反；C（呼吁忙碌的人照顾自己）未提及；D（帮助忙碌的人理清优先级）是短文提到的观点，但并非发起运动的目的，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文核心目的捕捉，关注开头明确提出的意图。"},{"no":17,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"17","options":{"A":"Avoid saying we are busy.","B":"Reflect on their true purpose","C":"Describeour schedule in detail.","D":"Respond as most busy people do."},"script":"I met three different people today, and each time, when I asked, \"How are you?\" The reply was exactly the same.\n**I'm busy\".Honestly, I hear the same answer from the vast majority of people I meet. So I started to think: \"Guess\nwhat? Everybody's busy!\" I'm busy, you're busy, everybody's busy. So you being busy doesn't make me sympathetic\nat all.Because\"busy\"is the state of the world.\n[16] So I'm launching a campaign to stop people complaining about being \"busy.\" It may sound harsh, but the truth is, nobody cares. In a busy world, \"being busy\" doesn't stand out, nor does it mean productive, creative,\naccomplished,or professional.\nSteve Maraboli once said, *When someone tells you they are too \"busy,it's not a reflection of their schedule: it's\na reflection of YOUR spot on their schedule.\"Donna Lynn Hope has also remarked, \"Busy doesn't mean better. I've\nnever envied a busy person—the one who likes to point out their busy lives to others.\"\nThe reality is, nobody is too busy-it's just about priorities. [17] So the next time someone asks you how you are,\nmaybe respond differently.[18] In my experience, the \"I'm busy\"response is really covering up the fact that they're not\nactually accomplishing their real purpose. And \"being busy\"is the lie they tell themselves about why they can't achieve it.","audioStart":867.8,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"A","expl":"Avoid saying we are busy. 详解：短文提到“下次有人问你‘你好吗？’，也许可以换一种方式回答”，结合前文“人人都回答‘我很忙’”，可推断换方式即避免说“我很忙”，选项A与原文一致。B（反思他们的真正目的）、C（详细描述日程）、D（像大多数忙碌的人一样回应）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文建议类信息捕捉，注意上下文逻辑关联。"},{"no":18,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"18","options":{"A":"To show their achievements resulted fromgreat efforts.","B":"To prove they stand out as accomplished professionals.","C":"To cover up their failure to achieve some purpose","D":"To tell others a complete lie about their inability."},"script":"I met three different people today, and each time, when I asked, \"How are you?\" The reply was exactly the same.\n**I'm busy\".Honestly, I hear the same answer from the vast majority of people I meet. So I started to think: \"Guess\nwhat? Everybody's busy!\" I'm busy, you're busy, everybody's busy. So you being busy doesn't make me sympathetic\nat all.Because\"busy\"is the state of the world.\n[16] So I'm launching a campaign to stop people complaining about being \"busy.\" It may sound harsh, but the truth is, nobody cares. In a busy world, \"being busy\" doesn't stand out, nor does it mean productive, creative,\naccomplished,or professional.\nSteve Maraboli once said, *When someone tells you they are too \"busy,it's not a reflection of their schedule: it's\na reflection of YOUR spot on their schedule.\"Donna Lynn Hope has also remarked, \"Busy doesn't mean better. I've\nnever envied a busy person—the one who likes to point out their busy lives to others.\"\nThe reality is, nobody is too busy-it's just about priorities. [17] So the next time someone asks you how you are,\nmaybe respond differently.[18] In my experience, the \"I'm busy\"response is really covering up the fact that they're not\nactually accomplishing their real purpose. And \"being busy\"is the lie they tell themselves about why they can't achieve it.","audioStart":867.8,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"C","expl":"To cover up their failure to achieve some purpose. 详解：短文结尾提到“根据我的经验，‘我很忙’这个回答其实是在掩盖一个事实：他们并没有实现自己真正的目标。‘忙碌’是他们对自己说的谎言，用来解释为什么无法实现目标”，选项C与原文一致。A（表明成就源于努力）、B（证明自己是杰出的专业人士）未提及；D（向他人撒谎说自己无能）错误，谎言是对自己说的，并非针对他人，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文深层原因捕捉，关注作者对现象的解读。"},{"no":19,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"19","options":{"A":"They will help one reduce fear","B":"They should become so popular.","C":"They will be less challenging after a time.","D":"They should cover so many different types"},"script":"More and more people are taking up extreme sports, like snowboarding, skateboarding,mountain biking, and\nmany more. Why are these sports becoming so popular?\nOne thing that extreme sports do better than perhaps anything else is to help you stop being afraid.[19] It may sound\nstrange to say that their main function is to reduce fear. But after a time, your fear response to other things in life will be\ndramatically reduced. After all, if you can jump out of a plane or off a bridge, then you can face anything else easily.\n[2o] When doing extreme sports, you have to become more focused. You'll be pushed to your limits, and if you\naren't focused you'll make dangerous mistakes. Learning to be this focused when enjoying extreme sports will help you\nto be focused at work, keeping you more productive and ultimately more successful.\nIt's great to stay fit and healthy, but standard exercise routines and sports only work the same muscles repeatedly.\n[2i] With extreme sports, you'll be working entirely different muscles, and that means you get an all-over workout.\nExtreme sports also burn a lot more calories than other sports. Skateboarding, for example, can burn as many as 500\ncalories per hour.Basketball burns around 300 in the same time.\nWhen you find that you can overcome the physical or mental challenges involved in extreme sports, you'll feel\nsuperhuman and your self-confidence will be at an all-time high.","audioStart":1034.6,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"A","expl":"They will help one reduce fear. 详解：短文提到“极限运动可能比其他任何运动都更能帮助你克服恐惧。说它们的主要功能是减少恐惧，听起来可能有些奇怪，但一段时间后，你对生活中其他事情的恐惧反应会大幅降低”，选项A与原文一致。B（应该大受欢迎）是短文开头的现象，并非“听起来奇怪”的内容；C（一段时间后挑战性降低）未提及；D（应该涵盖多种类型）是极限运动的特点，并非“听起来奇怪”的内容，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文特殊观点捕捉，注意转折连词后的意外信息。"},{"no":20,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"20","options":{"A":"To break one's ultimate limits.","B":"To stop being extremely afraid","C":"To avoid dangerous mistakes.","D":"To enjoy the sports to the full."},"script":"More and more people are taking up extreme sports, like snowboarding, skateboarding,mountain biking, and\nmany more. Why are these sports becoming so popular?\nOne thing that extreme sports do better than perhaps anything else is to help you stop being afraid.[19] It may sound\nstrange to say that their main function is to reduce fear. But after a time, your fear response to other things in life will be\ndramatically reduced. After all, if you can jump out of a plane or off a bridge, then you can face anything else easily.\n[2o] When doing extreme sports, you have to become more focused. You'll be pushed to your limits, and if you\naren't focused you'll make dangerous mistakes. Learning to be this focused when enjoying extreme sports will help you\nto be focused at work, keeping you more productive and ultimately more successful.\nIt's great to stay fit and healthy, but standard exercise routines and sports only work the same muscles repeatedly.\n[2i] With extreme sports, you'll be working entirely different muscles, and that means you get an all-over workout.\nExtreme sports also burn a lot more calories than other sports. Skateboarding, for example, can burn as many as 500\ncalories per hour.Basketball burns around 300 in the same time.\nWhen you find that you can overcome the physical or mental challenges involved in extreme sports, you'll feel\nsuperhuman and your self-confidence will be at an all-time high.","audioStart":1034.6,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"C","expl":"To avoid dangerous mistakes. 详解：短文提到“进行极限运动时，你必须更加专注。你会被推到极限，如果不集中注意力，就会犯下危险的错误”，选项C与原文一致。A（打破自身极限）是极限运动的客观结果，并非专注的原因；B（不再极度恐惧）是极限运动的整体益处，并非专注的直接原因；D（充分享受运动乐趣）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文因果关系捕捉，注意“if not”引导的反向逻辑。"},{"no":21,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"21","options":{"A":"By allowing our motivation to be at an all-time high.","B":"By stopping us hurting the same muscles repeatedly.","C":"By burning as many as 300 calories per hour.","D":"By enabling us to get an all-over workout"},"script":"More and more people are taking up extreme sports, like snowboarding, skateboarding,mountain biking, and\nmany more. Why are these sports becoming so popular?\nOne thing that extreme sports do better than perhaps anything else is to help you stop being afraid.[19] It may sound\nstrange to say that their main function is to reduce fear. But after a time, your fear response to other things in life will be\ndramatically reduced. After all, if you can jump out of a plane or off a bridge, then you can face anything else easily.\n[2o] When doing extreme sports, you have to become more focused. You'll be pushed to your limits, and if you\naren't focused you'll make dangerous mistakes. Learning to be this focused when enjoying extreme sports will help you\nto be focused at work, keeping you more productive and ultimately more successful.\nIt's great to stay fit and healthy, but standard exercise routines and sports only work the same muscles repeatedly.\n[2i] With extreme sports, you'll be working entirely different muscles, and that means you get an all-over workout.\nExtreme sports also burn a lot more calories than other sports. Skateboarding, for example, can burn as many as 500\ncalories per hour.Basketball burns around 300 in the same time.\nWhen you find that you can overcome the physical or mental challenges involved in extreme sports, you'll feel\nsuperhuman and your self-confidence will be at an all-time high.","audioStart":1034.6,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"D","expl":"By enabling us to get an all-over workout. 详解：短文提到“常规锻炼和运动只是反复锻炼同一块肌肉，而极限运动能锻炼到完全不同的肌肉，这意味着你会得到全面的锻炼”，选项D与原文一致。A（让积极性达到顶峰）错误，原文是“自信心达到顶峰”；B（阻止重复损伤同一块肌肉）未提及，原文仅说常规运动反复锻炼同一块肌肉，未提损伤；C（每小时燃烧300卡路里）是篮球的消耗，并非极限运动，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文对比类信息捕捉，注意极限运动与常规运动的差异。"},{"no":22,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"22","options":{"A":"It isnatural","B":"It is instrumental.","C":"It is personal","D":"It is vital."},"script":"[22] Most of us have been in teams or organizations where we've had conflict with the people that we're working\nwith around the ideas or decisions that we're discussing. Conflict is natural. We all bring different life and work\nexperiences to the table. We all have different personality preferences and tendencies. We're not all going to have the\nsame ideas on how to approach policies, programs or problems.\n[23] But too often we get caught in this place where conflict is perceived to be negative-something we want to\navoid so that we can maintain the harmony of our workplace. This could be because some people want to avoid conflict\nat all costs, after all they still have to work together. But this kind of artificial harmony isn't the answer.\n[24] Productive conflict is a vital part of teams and organizations that want to push forward and do more. Without\nconflict, we're often stuck in this artificial harmony where people don't express potentially innovative ideas for fear\nthat they may start conflict with others.\n[25] But if you're in a place where you have a basis of trust, conflict can be extremely productive. It can lead\nto increased innovation and greater trust on teams.It may be an uncomfortable process, but good leaders and healthy\nteams recognize that productive and healthy conflict is an important part of how they function.","audioStart":1211.0,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"A","expl":"It is natural. 详解：短文开头提到“我们大多数人都曾在团队或组织中，因讨论的想法或决策与同事发生冲突。冲突是正常的，因为我们都有不同的生活和工作经验、性格偏好”，选项A与原文一致。B（起重要作用）、D（至关重要）描述的是“富有成效的冲突”，而非所有冲突；C（是个人的）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文核心定义捕捉，关注开头对现象的定性描述。"},{"no":23,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"23","options":{"A":"To guard against being perceived negatively.","B":"To prevent themselves from being isolated.","C":"To conceal personality preferences.","D":"To maintain workplace harmony."},"script":"[22] Most of us have been in teams or organizations where we've had conflict with the people that we're working\nwith around the ideas or decisions that we're discussing. Conflict is natural. We all bring different life and work\nexperiences to the table. We all have different personality preferences and tendencies. We're not all going to have the\nsame ideas on how to approach policies, programs or problems.\n[23] But too often we get caught in this place where conflict is perceived to be negative-something we want to\navoid so that we can maintain the harmony of our workplace. This could be because some people want to avoid conflict\nat all costs, after all they still have to work together. But this kind of artificial harmony isn't the answer.\n[24] Productive conflict is a vital part of teams and organizations that want to push forward and do more. Without\nconflict, we're often stuck in this artificial harmony where people don't express potentially innovative ideas for fear\nthat they may start conflict with others.\n[25] But if you're in a place where you have a basis of trust, conflict can be extremely productive. It can lead\nto increased innovation and greater trust on teams.It may be an uncomfortable process, but good leaders and healthy\nteams recognize that productive and healthy conflict is an important part of how they function.","audioStart":1211.0,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"D","expl":"To maintain workplace harmony. 详解：短文提到“我们往往认为冲突是负面的，想要避免它以保持工作场所的和谐。这可能是因为有些人不惜一切代价避免冲突，毕竟他们还要一起工作”，选项D与原文一致。A（防止他人负面看待自己）、C（隐藏个性偏好）未提及；B（防止被孤立）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文原因类信息捕捉，注意“this could be because”引导的因果关系。"},{"no":24,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"24","options":{"A":"It helps to enhance team spirit","B":"It stimulates innovative ideas","C":"It helps to resolve problems.","D":"It facilitates policy-making."},"script":"[22] Most of us have been in teams or organizations where we've had conflict with the people that we're working\nwith around the ideas or decisions that we're discussing. Conflict is natural. We all bring different life and work\nexperiences to the table. We all have different personality preferences and tendencies. We're not all going to have the\nsame ideas on how to approach policies, programs or problems.\n[23] But too often we get caught in this place where conflict is perceived to be negative-something we want to\navoid so that we can maintain the harmony of our workplace. This could be because some people want to avoid conflict\nat all costs, after all they still have to work together. But this kind of artificial harmony isn't the answer.\n[24] Productive conflict is a vital part of teams and organizations that want to push forward and do more. Without\nconflict, we're often stuck in this artificial harmony where people don't express potentially innovative ideas for fear\nthat they may start conflict with others.\n[25] But if you're in a place where you have a basis of trust, conflict can be extremely productive. It can lead\nto increased innovation and greater trust on teams.It may be an uncomfortable process, but good leaders and healthy\nteams recognize that productive and healthy conflict is an important part of how they function.","audioStart":1211.0,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"B","expl":"详解：短文提到“富有成效的冲突是想要推进和做更多事情的团队和组织的重要组成部分。没有冲突，团队就会陷入人为的和谐，人们因害怕冲突而不敢表达潜在的创新想法”，说明富有成效的冲突能激发创新想法，选项B与原文一致。A（增强团队精神）、C（帮助解决问题）、D（有利于决策）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文重要性捕捉，关注“without”引导的反向论证。"},{"no":25,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"25","options":{"A":"An innovative mind.","B":"Corporate culture.","C":"Mutual trust.","D":"A healthy mentality. Part Ⅲ Reading Comprehension (40 minutes)"},"script":"[22] Most of us have been in teams or organizations where we've had conflict with the people that we're working\nwith around the ideas or decisions that we're discussing. Conflict is natural. We all bring different life and work\nexperiences to the table. We all have different personality preferences and tendencies. We're not all going to have the\nsame ideas on how to approach policies, programs or problems.\n[23] But too often we get caught in this place where conflict is perceived to be negative-something we want to\navoid so that we can maintain the harmony of our workplace. This could be because some people want to avoid conflict\nat all costs, after all they still have to work together. But this kind of artificial harmony isn't the answer.\n[24] Productive conflict is a vital part of teams and organizations that want to push forward and do more. Without\nconflict, we're often stuck in this artificial harmony where people don't express potentially innovative ideas for fear\nthat they may start conflict with others.\n[25] But if you're in a place where you have a basis of trust, conflict can be extremely productive. It can lead\nto increased innovation and greater trust on teams.It may be an uncomfortable process, but good leaders and healthy\nteams recognize that productive and healthy conflict is an important part of how they function.","audioStart":1211.0,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"C","expl":"Mutual trust. 详解：短文结尾提到“如果你处于一个有信任基础的地方，冲突可以非常富有成效。它能带来更多创新和更强的团队信任”，选项C（相互信任）是富有成效的冲突的基础，与原文一致。A（创新意识）是冲突带来的结果，并非基础；B（企业文化）、D（健康心态）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文前提条件捕捉，关注“if”引导的条件句。"},{"no":26,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 26 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"ultimate","answer":"O","expl":"ultimate 详解： 语法判断：空格前是名词短语“the broader picture”和连词“and”，空格后是名词短语“purpose of action”，空格处需填入形容词修饰“purpose”。 语义判断：本段介绍“交际抽象”，抽象语言的特点是“关注大局和行动的最终目标，而非细节和行动手段”，“ultimate purpose”意为“最终目标”，符合语境。 涉及知识点：形容词辨析及语义搭配，重点掌握“ultimate”的含义及用法。"},{"no":27,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 27 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"attaining","answer":"B","expl":"attaining 详解： 语法判断：空格前是介词“of”，空格后是名词“action”，空格处需填入分词或形容词修饰“action”。 语义判断：句子对比抽象语言和具象语言，具象语言关注“细节和实现行动的手段”，“attaining action”意为“实现行动”，符合语境。 涉及知识点：分词辨析及语义搭配，重点掌握“attaining”（实现、获得）的含义及用法。"},{"no":28,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 28 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"conclusion","answer":"C","expl":"详解： 语法判断：空格前是代词“this”，空格后是逗号，空格处需填入名词。 语义判断：上文提出研究结论（男性更倾向于抽象表达），本段介绍研究过程，“arrive at this conclusion”意为“得出这一结论”，符合语境。 涉及知识点：名词辨析及固定搭配，重点掌握“arrive at a conclusion”的用法。"},{"no":29,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 29 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"approximately","answer":"A","expl":"详解： 语法判断：空格所在句子主谓宾结构完整（the researchers computed abstractness ratings for 40,000 commonly used words），空格处需填入副词修饰数量“40,000”。 语义判断：句子意为“研究人员计算了大约4万个英语常用单词的抽象性等级”，“approximately”（大约）符合语境。 涉及知识点：副词辨析及数量修饰，重点掌握“approximately”的含义及用法。"},{"no":30,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 30 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"difficult","answer":"D","expl":"difficult 详解： 语法判断：空格前是副词“more”，空格后是不定式“to visualize”，空格处需填入形容词，构成“be difficult to do sth.”结构。 语义判断：上文提到具体单词（如桌子、椅子）容易想象，而抽象单词（如正义、道德）难以想象，“more difficult to visualize”意为“更难以想象”，符合语境。 涉及知识点：形容词辨析及固定句型，重点掌握“be difficult to do sth.”的用法。"},{"no":31,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 31 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"significantly","answer":"M","expl":"详解： 语法判断：空格所在句子主谓宾结构完整（men used more abstract language），空格处需填入副词修饰形容词比较级“more”。 语义判断：句子意为“男性在博客文章中明显使用了更多的抽象语言”，“significantly”（显著地、明显地）符合语境。 涉及知识点：副词辨析及比较级修饰，重点掌握“significantly”的含义及用法。"},{"no":32,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 32 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"source","answer":"N","expl":"source 详解： 语法判断：空格前是定冠词“the”，空格后是介词短语“of this effect”，空格处需填入名词。 语义判断：上文提出“男性更倾向于抽象表达”这一结果，本段追溯原因，“the source of this effect”意为“这一结果的根源”，符合语境。 涉及知识点：名词辨析及语义搭配，重点掌握“source”（根源、原因）的含义及用法。"},{"no":33,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 33 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"manipulated","answer":"I","expl":"详解： 语法判断：空格前是主语“the researchers”，空格后是宾语“power dynamics”，空格所在句子时态为过去时，空格处需填入动词过去式作谓语。 语义判断：前文提出“权力差异可能是关键因素”，本句通过实验验证：“研究人员在人际场景中操纵权力动态，观察其是否影响交际抽象”，“manipulated”（操纵、控制）符合语境。 涉及知识点：动词辨析及语义搭配，重点掌握“manipulated”的含义及过去式形式。"},{"no":34,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 34 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"fixed","answer":"F","expl":"fixed 详解： 语法判断：空格前是不定冠词“a”，空格后是名词“tendency”，空格处需填入形容词修饰“tendency”。 语义判断：实验结果表明，抽象交流并非男性或女性的固定趋势，而是受环境影响，“fixed tendency”意为“固定趋势”，符合语境。 涉及知识点：形容词辨析及语义搭配，重点掌握“fixed”（固定的）的含义及用法。"},{"no":35,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 35 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"emerges","answer":"E","expl":"emerges 详解： 语法判断：空格位于“not...but rather...”结构中，前后成分并列，前面是“does not reflect”，后面需填入动词第三人称单数形式。 语义判断：句子意为“抽象交流并非固定趋势，而是在特定环境中出现”，“emerges”（出现、显现）符合语境。 涉及知识点：动词辨析及主谓一致，重点掌握“emerges”的含义及第三人称单数形式。"},{"no":36,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"36. Our brains are genetically determined to satisfy immediate desires.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"I","expl":"题干“基因决定了我们的大脑要满足即时的欲望”对应I段第三句“我们的大脑天生就喜欢即时满足感”。题干中“genetically determined”对应原文“hard-wired”，“immediate desires”对应“instant gratification”，是对原文的同义转述，故答案为I。 涉及知识点：同义转述识别，重点关注词汇替换（genetically determined = hard-wired）。"},{"no":37,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"37. Taken in a practical way,saving for post-work years is like givingmoney away to others","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"C","expl":"题干“从实际来看，为退休后的生活存钱就像把钱送给别人一样”对应C段。该段首句提到“从实际来说，想到未来的自己时，大脑对他的印象就像对陌生人一样”，第二句进一步说明“为退休储蓄相当于把钱完全送给别人”。题干中“Taken in a practical way”对应原文“Put in practical terms”，“saving for post-work years”对应“saving for retirement”，是对原文的同义转述，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：同义转述识别，重点关注语义逻辑一致。"},{"no":38,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"38. Research found that,as regards achievement of one's goals,it is important to focus more on enjoying the process than the long-termbenefits","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"M","expl":"题干“研究发现，就个人目标的实现而言，更重要的是享受过程本身，而非着眼于长期的利益”对应M段首句“这些发现表明，在实现目标这件事上，享受过程本身比追求长期利益更为重要”。题干中“Research found that”对应原文“These findings suggest that”，“as regards achievement of one's goals”对应“when it comes to achieving your goals”，是对原文的概括总结，故答案为M。 涉及知识点：主旨概括识别，重点关注段落核心观点。"},{"no":39,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"39. Regarding our future selves as still being ourselves will help us make better long-term decisions","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"F","expl":"题干“把未来的自己视作仍然是自己，将帮助我们做出更好的长期决策”对应F段。该段提到拖延症的第二种解决方案：“让现在的自己相信，未来的自己实际上还是你，试图更加认同未来的自己将鼓励我们做出更好的长期决策”。题干是对该部分内容的概括总结，故答案为F。 涉及知识点：核心观点提炼，重点关注解决方案类信息。"},{"no":40,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"40. Savings rates in America have dropped in recent decades even though people's life expectancy has increased.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"A","expl":"题干“尽管人们的预期寿命有所延长，但近几十年来美国的储蓄有所下降”对应A段。该段第二句提到“人们的寿命在延长”，第三句转折指出“近几十年来美国的储蓄率却不升反降”。题干中“Savings rates in America have dropped”对应原文“savings rates in the U.S. have gone down”，“people's life expectancy has increased”对应“people are living longer”，是对原文的同义转述，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：转折关系及同义转述识别，重点关注“however”后的关键信息。"},{"no":41,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"41. Researchers found that enjoyment rather than importance enabledpeople to persist in their goals.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"K","expl":"题干“研究人员发现，相比目标的重要性，享受过程更能促使人们坚持追求目标”对应K段最后一句“我们发现，与他们对目标重要性的评价相比，过程的享受更能预测目标设定两个月后人们的坚持情况”。题干中“Researchers found that”对应原文“We found that”，“enjoyment rather than importance enabled people to persist in their goals”对应“enjoyment predicted people's goal persistence far more than how important they rated their goal to be”，是对原文的同义转述，故答案为K。 涉及知识点：比较关系及同义转述识别，重点关注“more than”引导的比较逻辑。"},{"no":42,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"42. When making decisions,we give priority to our current frame of mind without thinking much of the consequences.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"E","expl":"题干“在做决定时，我们优先考虑当前的心境，而不太考虑后果”对应E段最后一句“但在这样做（即做决定）的过程中，我们优先考虑的是我们当前的情绪，而不是我们的不作为会给未来的自己带来的后果”。题干中“When making decisions”对应原文“in doing so”，“give priority to our current frame of mind without thinking much of the consequences”对应“prioritize our current mood over the consequences of our inaction for the future self”，是对原文的同义转述，故答案为E。 涉及知识点：同义转述识别，重点关注情绪与后果的对比逻辑。"},{"no":43,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"43. People ate more ofa healthy food when they focused on its good taste instead ofits long-term benefits","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"L","expl":"题干“当人们专注于健康食品的美味而非其长期的益处时，他们吃得更多”对应L段第二句“当人们被引导专注于食物的美味而非长期的健康益处时，他们摄入的健康食品量会增加50%”。题干中“People ate more of a healthy food”对应原文“people ate 50% more of a healthy food”，“focused on its good taste instead of its long-term benefits”对应“directed to focus on the good taste rather than the long-term health benefits”，是对原文的同义转述，故答案为L。 涉及知识点：同义转述识别，重点关注数量变化与条件的关联。"},{"no":44,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"44. As was expected,when people thought of their present selves,their brains were observed to become more active","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"B","expl":"题干“正如预期的那样，当人们想到当前的自己时，（研究人员）观察到他们的大脑变得更加活跃”对应B段第三句“不出所料，人们在思考当前的自己时大脑最活跃”。题干中“As was expected”对应原文“Unsurprisingly”，“when people thought of their present selves”对应“when thinking about their current selves”，“their brains were observed to become more active”对应“people's brains were most active”，是对原文的同义转述，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：同义转述识别，重点关注实验结果的描述。"},{"no":45,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"45. Researchers found that","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"G","expl":"题干“研究人员发现，与没有看到自己年老时的形象的参与者相比，看到自己年老时的形象的参与者会为晚年存更多的钱”对应G段最后一句“看到年老时的自己的参与者表示，他们会比对照组为退休多存30%的工资”。题干中“participants who saw the images of their aged selves”对应原文“Participants who saw their aged selves”，“would save more for their later years than those who didn't”对应“would save 30% more of their salary for retirement than the control group”，是对原文的概括总结，故答案为G。 涉及知识点：对比关系及概括总结，重点关注实验分组及结果差异。"},{"no":46,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"46. What does the author say we need to do to strengthen our willpower?","options":{"A":"Keep it under control.","B":"Apply it continuously.","C":"Learn from entrepreneurs","D":"Aim at success determinedly."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"B","expl":"详解：题干问“作者说我们需要做什么来增强意志力”，定位到第二段第一句“意志力就像肌肉一样；要想保持强大的意志力，就必须不断地加以锻炼”。“不断锻炼”即“持续运用”，选项B与原文一致。A（控制它）、C（向企业家学习）、D（坚定地以成功为目标）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解，重点关注比喻修辞后的核心观点（意志力=肌肉，需持续锻炼）。"},{"no":47,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"47. How are almost half of ourdaily actions performed according to the passage?","options":{"A":"Out of habit.","B":"With determination.","C":"Like muscle building.","D":"By self-discipline"},"passage":"passage1","answer":"A","expl":"Out of habit. 详解：题干问“根据文章，我们几乎一半的日常行为是怎样做出的”，定位到第三段第三句“我们的日常行为当中几乎有一半是源于我们的习惯，而不是决定”。选项A（出于习惯）与原文一致，B（下定决心）、C（像肌肉锻炼一样）、D（通过自律）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解，重点关注“rather than”引导的对比信息。"},{"no":48,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"48. What will help people stick to doing something constructive automatically?","options":{"A":"Practising it on a day-to-day basis","B":"Associating it with improving health.","C":"Possessing a reasonable amount of talent","D":"Foreseeing the desired outcome it will yield."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"D","expl":"详解：题干问“什么将会帮助人们自然而然地坚持做一些有助益的事情”，定位到第四段最后一句“将一个习惯和有益的结果关联起来，会有助于你长久地坚持这个习惯”。“有益的结果”即“理想结果”，选项D（预见它将产生的理想结果）与原文一致。A（每天练习）是坚持习惯的方式，并非“帮助自然坚持”的原因；B（与改善健康联系起来）是原文例子，并非普遍情况；C（具备一定天赋）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解及同义替换，重点掌握“valuable outcome”与“desired outcome”的替换。"},{"no":49,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"49. How does the art of self-control help us succeed?","options":{"A":"By allowing us to remain clear-headed permanently.","B":"By enabling us to alter our behaviors constantly.","C":"By enabling us to takepositive actions","D":"By allowing us toavoid taking risks."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"C","expl":"By enabling us to take positive actions. 详解：题干问“自制力这门艺术是如何帮助我们成功的”，定位到倒数第二段第二句“自制力能让你避免做出那些无助于你成功的行为，而让你采取那些有助于你成功的行为”。“采取有助于成功的行为”即“采取积极的行动”，选项C与原文一致。A（永远保持头脑清醒）、B（不断改变行为）、D（避免冒险）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解及同义替换，重点掌握“adopt those that contribute to your success”与“take positive actions”的替换。"},{"no":50,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"50. Why can it be difficult for us to maintain self-control?","options":{"A":"Most ofus are not in the habit of exercising self-control.","B":"We may not get immediate rewards from self-control.","C":"Self-control tends to be associated with pains.","D":"Self-control only brings about small benefits"},"passage":"passage1","answer":"B","expl":"We may not get immediate rewards from self-control. 详解：题干问“为什么我们很难保持自制力”，定位到最后一段第一句“因为自制力带来的满足感常常是延迟的，所以很容易偏离轨道”。“满足感延迟”即“无法获得立竿见影的回报”，选项B与原文一致。A（大多数人没有锻炼自制力的习惯）、C（自制力往往与痛苦有关）、D（自制力只能带来微小的益处）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解及同义替换，重点掌握“delayed satisfaction”与“no immediate rewards”的替换。"},{"no":51,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"51. What does the passage mainly discuss regarding scientific research?","options":{"A":"Its foundation.","B":"Its rewards.","C":"Its prospect","D":"Itsfunding"},"passage":"passage2","answer":"D","expl":"Its funding. 详解：题干问“文章主要讨论了科学研究的什么”，属于主旨题。第一段第一句“如今，大多数科学研究都是由政府拨款、研发公司以及非营利性基金会资助的”开篇点题，全文围绕科学研究的资金来源、历史变化及资金可能带来的偏见展开，选项D（资金）符合主旨。A（基础）、B（回报）、C（前景）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：主旨大意归纳，重点关注开篇主题句及全文论述脉络。"},{"no":52,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"52. What do we learn from the passage about researchers like birdwatchers and rock collectors?","options":{"A":"They have little access to government funding.","B":"They can do research with limited resources.","C":"They can do amateur work in their own fields.","D":"They have no means for large-scale research."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"B","expl":"They can do research with limited resources. 详解：题干问“关于鸟类观察者和岩石收集者等研究人员，我们能了解到什么”，定位到第二段最后一句“鸟类观察者、岩石收藏者和其他人可以在有限的预算下进行真正的研究”。“有限的预算”即“有限的资源”，选项B与原文一致。A（几乎无法获得政府资助）、C（可以在自己领域从事业余工作）未提及；D（无法进行大规模研究）错误，原文仅说他们进行小规模研究，未说无法进行大规模研究，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解及同义替换，重点掌握“limited budget”与“limited resources”的替换。"},{"no":53,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"53. What would scientific studies look like in a perfect world according to the author?","options":{"A":"They would be totally unbiased.","B":"They would be independently funded.","C":"They would be responsibly conducted.","D":"They would be strictly supervised."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"A","expl":"They would be totally unbiased. 详解：题干问“根据作者的说法，在完美的世界里，科学研究会是什么样子的”，定位到第三段第一句“在一个完美的世界里，金钱并不会成为问题——所有的科学研究都是完全客观的”。“完全客观”即“完全公正、无偏见”，选项A与原文一致。B（获得独立资助）、C（负责任地进行）、D（受到严格监督）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解及同义替换，重点掌握“objective”与“unbiased”的替换。"},{"no":54,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"54. What does the author say about companies and special interest groups?","options":{"A":"They try hard to pull down the safeguards for research.","B":"They make extra efforts to research their own products.","C":"They provide valuable resources for scientific research.","D":"They reap the most benefits from scientific research"},"passage":"passage2","answer":"C","expl":"详解：题干问“关于公司和特殊利益团体，作者说了什么”，定位到最后一段第三、四句“当然不是。这些团体为科学研究提供了极为宝贵的资金”。“宝贵的资金”即“宝贵的资源”，选项C与原文一致。A（竭力破坏研究保障措施）、B（额外努力研究自己的产品）、D（从科学研究中获益最多）未提及，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解及同义替换，重点掌握“invaluable funding”与“valuable resources”的替换。"},{"no":55,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"55. What does the author think ofresearch funded by industry or special interest groups?","options":{"A":"Its recommendations should be examined for feasibility.","B":"Its misleading results should be corrected in time.","C":"Its validity should be checked with additional care.","D":"Its hidden biases should be identified independently."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"C","expl":"详解：题干问“作者如何看待由行业或特殊利益团体资助的研究”，定位到最后一段倒数第三句“与此同时，要特别谨慎地审查由行业或特殊利益团体资助的研究，这是值得的”。“特别谨慎地审查”即“格外谨慎地检查其有效性”，选项C与原文一致。A（审查其建议的可行性）、D（独立识别其隐藏的偏见）未提及；B（及时纠正其误导性结果）是客观过程，并非作者的看法，故排除。 涉及知识点：细节理解及同义替换，重点掌握“examine with extra care”与“check validity with additional care”的替换。"}],"warnings":[]};